window.docContents[7].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Path-algebra-of-a-finite-quiver-has-a-unit/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Path-algebra-of-a-finite-quiver-has-a-unit":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers","header":"Path algebra of a finite quiver has a unit","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Path-algebra-of-a-finite-quiver-has-a-unit/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___8___-Quivers--Path-algebra-of-a-finite-quiver-has-a-unit"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Quiver-representations-are-easier-in-practice/#Chapter2___Remark2___8___7":{"contents":"Remark 2.8.7. In practice, it is generally easier to consider a representation of a quiver as in Definition 2.8.3.\n\nWe lastly define several previous concepts in the context of quiver representations.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Quiver representations are easier in practice","header":"Quiver representations are easier in practice","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Quiver-representations-are-easier-in-practice/#Chapter2___Remark2___8___7"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Homomorphism-of-Lie-algebras/#Chapter2___Definition2___9___6___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009Homomorphism of Lie algebras\u0009Homomorphism of Lie algebras","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Homomorphism-of-Lie-algebras/#Chapter2___Definition2___9___6___formalization"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___12___-Problems/Section-4___12___-Problems/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___12___-Problems--Section-4___12___-Problems":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.12. Problems","header":"Section 4.12: Problems","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___12___-Problems/Section-4___12___-Problems/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___12___-Problems--Section-4___12___-Problems"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/5___25___3___-Principal-series/#Chapter5___Discussion_5___25___3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.25. Representations of $GL_2(\\mathbb{F}_q)$\u00095.25.3. Principal series\u0009Section 5.25.3: Principal series representations — setup of B, V_{lambda_1,lambda_2}\u0009Section 5.25.3: Principal series representations — setup of B, V_{lambda_1,lambda_2}","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___25___-Representations-of-___GL_2_LPAR____mathbb___F____q_RPAR____/5___25___3___-Principal-series/#Chapter5___Discussion_5___25___3___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Proof-of-Artin___s-theorem-_LPAR_both-directions_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___26___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.26. Artin's theorem\u0009Proof of Artin's theorem (both directions)\u0009Proof of Artin's theorem (both directions)","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Proof-of-Artin___s-theorem-_LPAR_both-directions_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5___26___1___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/Minimal-polynomial___-algebraic-conjugates___-and-Vieta___s-theorem/#Chapter5___Discussion_after_Proposition5___2___5":{"contents":"Every algebraic number \\alpha has a minimal polynomial p(x) which is the monic polynomial with rational coefficients of the smallest degree such that p(\\alpha) = 0. Any other polynomial q(x) with rational coefficients such that q(\\alpha) = 0 is divisible by p(x). Roots of p(x) are called the algebraic conjugates of \\alpha; they are roots of any polynomial q with rational coefficients such that q(\\alpha) = 0.\n\nNote that any algebraic conjugate of an algebraic integer is obviously also an algebraic integer. Therefore, by the Vieta theorem, the minimal polynomial of an algebraic integer has integer coefficients.\n\nBelow we will need the following lemma:\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.2. Algebraic numbers and algebraic integers\u0009Minimal polynomial, algebraic conjugates, and Vieta's theorem","header":"Minimal polynomial, algebraic conjugates, and Vieta's theorem","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/Minimal-polynomial___-algebraic-conjugates___-and-Vieta___s-theorem/#Chapter5___Discussion_after_Proposition5___2___5"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Problem-6___1___2___-Some-algebraic-geometry/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___1___-Problems--Problem-6___1___2___-Some-algebraic-geometry":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.1. Problems","header":"Problem 6.1.2: Some algebraic geometry","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___1___-Problems/Problem-6___1___2___-Some-algebraic-geometry/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___1___-Problems--Problem-6___1___2___-Some-algebraic-geometry"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Proposition-6___6___8___-Dimension-vector-under-reflection-is-s_i_LPAR_d_LPAR_V_RPAR__RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___6___-Reflection-functors--Proposition-6___6___8___-Dimension-vector-under-reflection-is-s_i_LPAR_d_LPAR_V_RPAR__RPAR_--Proposition-6___6___8___-Dimension-vector-under-reflection-is-s_i_LPAR_d_LPAR_V_RPAR__RPAR_--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"If the displayed map is injective, the integer cast of the conclusion's natural-valued expression equals its other displayed integer-valued expression at every vertex.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Proposition6.6.8; role=primary]\n\nIf the displayed map is surjective, the integer cast of the conclusion's natural-valued expression equals its other displayed integer-valued expression at every vertex.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Proposition6.6.8; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.6. Reflection functors\u0009Proposition 6.6.8: Dimension vector under reflection is s_i(d(V))\u0009Proposition 6.6.8: Dimension vector under reflection is s_i(d(V))\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Proposition-6___6___8___-Dimension-vector-under-reflection-is-s_i_LPAR_d_LPAR_V_RPAR__RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___6___-Reflection-functors--Proposition-6___6___8___-Dimension-vector-under-reflection-is-s_i_LPAR_d_LPAR_V_RPAR__RPAR_--Proposition-6___6___8___-Dimension-vector-under-reflection-is-s_i_LPAR_d_LPAR_V_RPAR__RPAR_--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___2___-Functors/Section-7___2___-Functors/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___2___-Functors--Section-7___2___-Functors":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.2. Functors","header":"Section 7.2: Functors","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___2___-Functors/Section-7___2___-Functors/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___2___-Functors--Section-7___2___-Functors"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/Section-7___5___-Representable-functors/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___5___-Representable-functors--Section-7___5___-Representable-functors--Section-7___5___-Representable-functors--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Declaration: CategoryTheory.Functor.IsCorepresentable\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter7/Introduction_7.5; role=supporting\n\nDeclaration: CategoryTheory.Functor.IsRepresentable\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter7/Introduction_7.5; role=supporting\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.5. Representable functors\u0009Section 7.5: Representable functors\u0009Section 7.5: Representable functors\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___5___-Representable-functors/Section-7___5___-Representable-functors/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___5___-Representable-functors--Section-7___5___-Representable-functors--Section-7___5___-Representable-functors--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Complex___-differentials___-cohomology___-exact-sequence/#Chapter7___Definition7___8___1":{"contents":"Definition 7.8.1. A sequence of objects C_i, i \\in \\mathbb{Z}, of an abelian category \\mathcal{C} and morphisms d_i : C_i \\to C_{i+1} is said to be a complex if the composition of any two consecutive arrows is zero. The morphisms d_i are called the differentials.A famous example of a complex is the de Rham complex, in which C_m is the space of differential m-forms on a Euclidean space or, more generally, a manifold, and d_m is the exterior differential of differential forms. This explains the term \"differential\".\nH^i = \\operatorname{Ker}(d_i)/\\operatorname{Im}(d_{i-1}). The complex is said to be exact in the ith term if H^i = 0 and is said to be an exact sequence if it is exact in all terms. The cohomology of this complex is\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.8. Complexes and cohomology\u0009Complex, differentials, cohomology, exact sequence","header":"Complex, differentials, cohomology, exact sequence","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Complex___-differentials___-cohomology___-exact-sequence/#Chapter7___Definition7___8___1"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Discussion-after-Example-8___1___7-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules--Discussion-after-Example-8___1___7-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories--Discussion-after-Example-8___1___7-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"A property of an object in a category.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Definition8.1.8; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Discussion_after_Example8.1.7; role=supporting]\n\nA property of an object in a category.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Definition8.1.8; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Discussion_after_Example8.1.7; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.1. Projective and injective modules\u0009Discussion after Example 8.1.7 — Projective and injective objects in abelian categories\u0009Discussion after Example 8.1.7 — Projective and injective objects in abelian categories\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Discussion-after-Example-8___1___7-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules--Discussion-after-Example-8___1___7-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories--Discussion-after-Example-8___1___7-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/Lifting-complete-systems-of-orthogonal-idempotents/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents--Lifting-complete-systems-of-orthogonal-idempotents--Lifting-complete-systems-of-orthogonal-idempotents--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"A complete orthogonal family of idempotents in the quotient by a nilpotent two-sided ideal\nlifts to a complete orthogonal family in the ring.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Corollary9.1.3; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.1. Lifting of idempotents\u0009Lifting complete systems of orthogonal idempotents\u0009Lifting complete systems of orthogonal idempotents\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents/Lifting-complete-systems-of-orthogonal-idempotents/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___1___-Lifting-of-idempotents--Lifting-complete-systems-of-orthogonal-idempotents--Lifting-complete-systems-of-orthogonal-idempotents--Formalization--Primary-declarations"}});